\author{brentyorgey}
\date{2026-03-28T00:49:21Z}
\title{Disco: exhaustiveness checking for arithmetic patterns}

\p{A couple summers ago, my student [[colin-phillips]] implemented exhaustiveness checking for [[000N]], following [Lower your guards: a compositional pattern-match coverage checker](https://al.radbox.org/doi/10.1145/3408989) by Graf, Peyton Jones, and Scott.  So it can now do things like
\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> f : Unit + (Bool * Nat) -> Int
Disco> f(right(False, n)) = 3
Warning: the function f is undefined for some inputs. For example:
f(left(■)) = ...
f(right(true, 0)) = ...
f(right(true, 1)) = ...<<<
  However, Disco also has \em{arithmetic patterns}, like
\pre\verb<<<|
f : N -> N
f(2n) = n + 1
f(2n+1) = 3<<<
but doing coverage checking for these is tricky in general. Quick, for what values (if any) is the below (partial) function undefined?
\pre\verb<<<|
f : N -> N
f(3n) = 0
f(1+5n) = 1
f(2n+2) = 2
f(n+12) = 3<<<
My student [[katherine-reid]] was working on a library for solving this problem, via the theory of [semilinear sets](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Generalized_arithmetic_progression#Semilinear_sets), but we never quite finished incorporating it into the Disco coverage checker.  Since I'm using Disco to teach again this semester, I've started working on this again.  This week, I mostly just spent some time understanding the existing code and writing down a list of the reamining tasks.  I'm optimistic I can get it polished up and merged before the end of the semester.
  }

\p{(\em{Answer: the function is undefined for #{5} and #{7}.})}
